# The append-only streams (`data/log/*.jsonl`, `data/todos.jsonl`) used to be
# tracked and carried `merge=union`, so two machines appending different lines
# merged without ever raising a conflict. They are not tracked any more — the
# repo holds the app, not what you have done with it — so those rules would now
# apply to nothing, and are removed rather than left here to be believed.
#
# The *read-side* handling they relied on is still in place and still earns its
# keep, because duplicated and out-of-order lines can arrive from any direction
# and not only from a merge: events sort by `ts`, stably, so a `session, undo,
# session` inside one second keeps file order and resolves to *checked*, and the
# fold is last-wins throughout. `backend/tests/test_sync.py` still pins all of
# it, and should keep doing so.

* text=auto eol=lf

# The exception, and it is a real one rather than a preference. PowerShell reads
# a `.ps1` with the console's default encoding assumptions, and Windows tooling
# that edits these files — including the ISE and a few installers' log parsers —
# writes CRLF back, which would show as a whole-file diff every time. Attributes
# for these existed once, were deleted with the Windows installer, and are back
# for the same reason they were there the first time.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# These three files also carry a UTF-8 BOM, and must keep it. Windows
# PowerShell 5.1 — which is what `powershell.exe` still means, what the backup
# scheduled task runs and what `backup.py` shells out to — reads a `.ps1`
# without one as cp1252. Every em-dash in a comment then decodes to three
# characters, the last of which is a right curly quote, and PowerShell treats
# curly quotes as string delimiters: the file parses as one long unbalanced
# string and fails with "Missing closing '}'" pointing at a line nowhere near
# the problem. It is invisible under pwsh 7, which assumes UTF-8, and it fails
# or not depending on whether a file happens to hold an even number of dashes.
# Do not strip the BOM to make a diff tidy.
